<non_negotiables>
The worksheet must:
Be delivered as an actual PDF file
Fit on exactly one page
Use only black and white
Use a polished collegiate case-study handout style
Target intro-level college students who are still building confidence
Be application-based and collaborative
Take 15–20 minutes to complete
Focus on exactly one main concept within the given subtopic
Include exactly 3 numbered questions
Include a compact answer key locked to the bottom of the page
Include no student answer space except the NAME line
</non_negotiables>
<title_generation>
Generate a concise question name based on the scenario and questions.
The question name must:
Be generated by the AI
Not be provided as an input
Be specific to the scenario
Be 4–8 words long
Use Title Case
Sound like a collegiate case-study title
Prefer decision-oriented wording when appropriate
Avoid generic names such as “Practice Problem,” “Worksheet,” or “Case Study”
The centered title must use this exact format:
[subtopic]: [Generated Question Name]
Example:
Time Value of Money: Campus Laptop Financing Decision
</title_generation>
<page_structure>
Use this exact page order:
Top-left name line:
NAME: **************\_\_**************
Centered title:
[subtopic]: [Generated Question Name]
Thin horizontal rule beneath the title.
Short realistic scenario paragraph.
Optional compact table, chart, graph, or diagram only if it directly supports the problem.
Centered section heading:
QUESTIONS
Exactly 3 numbered questions, each with a bold mini-title.
Variable blank space if needed.
Bottom-anchored answer-key block:
Thin full-width horizontal rule
Centered heading: ANSWER KEY
Compact instructor solutions
The horizontal rule must belong to the answer-key block, not the question section.
</page_structure>
<header_requirements>
The header must follow this exact format:
Line 1:
Left aligned
Text must read exactly: NAME: **************\_\_**************
Font size: 9–10 pt
Line 2:
Center aligned
Text must follow this exact format: [subtopic]: [Generated Question Name]
Font size: 15–18 pt
Bold serif font or small caps
Title Case
Do not use a three-part header.
Do not place the subject in the header.
Do not place a case study number in the header.
Do not place the NAME line on the right side.
</header_requirements>
<typography_requirements>
Use academic serif typography.
Requirements:
Serif font such as Times New Roman, Latin Modern Roman, Computer Modern, or similar
Body text: 10–11 pt
Answer key text: 8.5–9.5 pt
Answer key text must not be more than 2 pt smaller than body text
Section headings: centered, bold, small caps
Question mini-titles: bold
Tight single spacing or 1.05–1.15 line spacing
Compact paragraph spacing
No oversized fonts
No excessive whitespace
Do not shrink any text below 8.5 pt to force content onto one page
</typography_requirements>
<math_formatting_requirements>
All mathematical notation in the final PDF must be professionally typeset.
Requirements:
Do not use caret notation for exponents in the final PDF.
Do not write expressions such as r^2, (1+r)^t, (1.07)^3, or x^-1.
Use true superscripts for exponents.
Correct examples:
r², not r^2
(1 + r)², not (1 + r)^2
(1.07)³, not (1.07)^3
(1.005)⁻¹², not (1.005)^-12
Use rendered math formatting for complex expressions.
Use stacked fractions when possible.
Use clean mathematical notation in both the questions and the answer key.
If using LaTeX internally to create the PDF, write formulas in LaTeX math mode and render them into the PDF.
The final PDF must show rendered math symbols, not raw LaTeX code.
</math_formatting_requirements>
<question_quality_requirements>
The worksheet must require about 15 minutes of real student work.
Design the questions so that:
A strong student cannot finish the full worksheet in under 8 minutes.
A struggling intro-level college student can still finish in 15–20 minutes with careful work.
The worksheet feels manageable but not obvious.
Students must think, set up the problem, calculate, and interpret.
The questions are not just formula substitution.
Use this difficulty progression:
Question 1: Set up the model or identify the relevant information. This should take about 4–5 minutes.
Question 2: Perform the main calculation or comparison. This should take about 5–6 minutes.
Question 3: Make a decision, interpret the result, test a change, or find a break-even value. This should take about 5–7 minutes.
Each numbered question may include up to 2 short subparts, labeled (a) and (b), only if needed to create appropriate 15-minute difficulty.
Do not create more than 6 total student tasks across the 3 questions.
If a question is too easy, add one useful subpart that requires:
A comparison
A justification
A second calculation step
A sensitivity check
A break-even calculation
An interpretation in context
Do not add difficulty by using messy numbers, advanced notation, or unrelated concepts.
</question_quality_requirements>
<worksheet_content_requirements>
Use one realistic everyday, professional, financial, scientific, engineering, or academic scenario.
The scenario must be:
Simple to understand
Meaningful enough for intro-level college work
Directly connected to the subject and subtopic
Not distracting
Not overly detailed
Rich enough to support 3 connected questions
Meaningful to the case, not just a numerical question with a story attached
All 3 questions must:
Build logically from the same scenario
Use the same main concept within the subtopic
Be clearly worded
Require students to reason about the context, not just compute mechanically
At least 2 questions must require 2 or more distinct mathematical steps.
A distinct mathematical step can include:
Identifying relevant information
Choosing the correct method or formula
Setting up an equation, model, table, comparison, or expression
Substituting values
Calculating
Comparing results
Interpreting the result
At least 1 question must require students to explain, justify, compare, interpret, or discuss their reasoning with a partner.
At least 1 question must include a decision, comparison, sensitivity check, break-even point, recommendation, or interpretation that depends on previous calculations.
</worksheet_content_requirements>
<difficulty_requirements>
Target intro-level college difficulty.
Do not create:
Basic high school review
A quick formula drill
Questions solved immediately by substituting one value into one formula
A worksheet that can be completed in under 8 minutes
A worksheet that requires advanced methods beyond the subtopic
Use clean, beginner-friendly numbers, but do not make the numbers trivial.
No numbered question may consist only of identifying values and substituting them into a single formula.
The worksheet must require enough thought, setup, calculation, interpretation, and discussion to fill 15–20 minutes.
Avoid unnecessary:
Algebraic complexity
Computational complexity
Advanced notation
Extra concepts outside the given subtopic
</difficulty_requirements>
<collaboration_requirement>
The collaboration question must be meaningful, not decorative.
At least one question must explicitly ask students to:
Compare answers with a partner
Defend a recommendation
Discuss how changing an assumption affects the result
Justify which option is better
Interpret what the result means in context
The collaboration prompt must require reasoning, not just agreement.
</collaboration_requirement>
<visual_and_table_style>
Use visuals only when they improve the worksheet.
A table, chart, graph, or diagram is recommended when the scenario includes multiple values, options, rates, quantities, or time periods.
Any table, chart, graph, or diagram must:
Be black and white
Be centered
Be compact
Use simple labels
Include only necessary information
Use black borders only if a table is included
Avoid shading, color, icons, clip art, and decorative graphics
</visual_and_table_style>
<question_format>
Format each question exactly like this:
Bold Mini-Title
Question text.
If the question has subparts, format them like this:
(a) First task.
(b) Second task.
The mini-title must describe the task, not the math topic alone.
Good examples:
Compare the Plans
Find the Break-Even Payment
Interpret the Decision
Build the Model
Test the Assumption
Avoid generic mini-titles:
Question 1
Calculation
Formula
</question_format>
<answer_key_placement>
The answer key must be locked to the bottom of the page.
Placement requirements:
Place the answer key as a bottom-anchored block.
Place one thin full-width horizontal rule immediately above the ANSWER KEY heading.
Do not place the horizontal rule immediately after Question 3 unless the answer key naturally begins there.
Allow variable blank space between the end of Question 3 and the horizontal rule.
Do not put any text, divider, box, or decoration after Question 3 before the variable blank space.
Do not use horizontal divider lines between individual answer-key solutions.
Do not box the answer key.
</answer_key_placement>
<answer_key_requirements>
The answer key must:
Appear under a centered ANSWER KEY heading
Match the numbering and subparts of the questions
Give the correct final answer for each question
Show only the minimum setup needed to verify correctness
Use professionally rendered mathematical notation
Use true superscripts for exponents
Use stacked fractions when possible
Never show raw caret notation such as ^2, ^t, or ^-12
Put each major calculation step on its own separate line
Put formula, substitution, simplification, and final answer on separate lines when space allows
Avoid long inline calculation chains
Avoid combining unrelated calculation steps in one sentence
Use compact notation where appropriate
Avoid long explanations
Remain readable and visually balanced
Fit within the bottom answer-key block
Required answer key format:
Mini-title
Setup or formula line
Substitution or computation line
Simplification line
Final answer line
If a question has subparts, label the answer key with matching (a) and (b).
If a question requires interpretation, put the interpretation on its own final line.
The answer key may use columns only if:
Each solution remains readable
Each calculation step stays on its own line
The font remains at least 8.5 pt
The layout does not look like fine print
Do not place horizontal lines between answer-key items.
</answer_key_requirements>
<page_utilization_requirements>
The worksheet content must occupy most of the usable page area.
Requirements:
Use approximately 80–95% of the usable page height.
The page must look full, balanced, and intentional.
Do not leave large blank areas at the bottom.
Do not create oversized margins or excessive spacing.
The answer key must remain locked to the bottom.
Any extra vertical space must appear between the end of Question 3 and the horizontal rule above the ANSWER KEY heading.
If the page is underfilled:
Add relevant scenario detail.
Add a useful compact visual.
Add a meaningful subpart to one question.
Add a decision, comparison, sensitivity check, break-even point, or interpretation layer.
If the page is crowded:
Shorten the scenario.
Condense question wording.
Simplify visuals.
Shorten answer-key explanations.
Use compact formatting.
Do not reduce answer-key font below 8.5 pt.
</page_utilization_requirements>
<quality_check>
Before producing the PDF, silently verify:
The output is an actual PDF file.
The worksheet fits on exactly one page.
The worksheet uses 80–95% of the usable page height.
The worksheet uses only black and white.
The worksheet has no student answer space except the NAME line.
The title uses Title Case and follows [subtopic]: [Generated Question Name].
The worksheet uses polished academic serif formatting.
The worksheet uses professionally rendered math notation.
No raw caret exponent notation appears anywhere in the PDF.
Exponents appear as true superscripts.
Fractions are typeset cleanly when possible.
The worksheet uses exactly one realistic scenario.
The worksheet focuses on exactly one main concept within the subtopic.
The worksheet includes exactly 3 numbered questions.
The worksheet has no more than 6 total student tasks, including subparts.
The questions follow a setup → calculation/comparison → decision/interpretation progression.
At least 2 questions require 2 or more distinct mathematical steps.
No question is only one-step formula substitution.
At least 1 question requires meaningful collaboration, explanation, comparison, justification, or interpretation.
At least 1 question includes a decision, comparison, sensitivity check, break-even point, recommendation, or interpretation based on prior work.
A strong student would need more than 8 minutes to complete the worksheet.
A struggling intro-level college student could complete the worksheet in 15–20 minutes.
The numbers are clean but not trivial.
The answer key is locked to the bottom of the page.
One thin horizontal rule appears immediately above the ANSWER KEY heading.
No horizontal lines appear between answer-key solutions.
The answer key is correct, compact, readable, and at the bottom.
Answer key font is 8.5–9.5 pt and does not look like fine print.
No learning objective appears anywhere on the worksheet.
No extra commentary appears before or after the PDF.
</quality_check>
<revision_rules>
If the worksheet is too easy, add one meaningful subpart or add one layer of reasoning, interpretation, comparison, sensitivity analysis, break-even analysis, or decision-making.
If the worksheet is too complex, simplify the scenario, reduce unnecessary steps, remove extra concepts, or make the numbers cleaner.
If the math notation uses raw caret exponents, regenerate the PDF with true superscripts or rendered math.
If raw LaTeX code appears in the PDF, regenerate the PDF so all formulas are rendered visually.
If the collaboration prompt is superficial, revise it so students must compare, defend, justify, or interpret a decision.
If the answer key is hard to read, split calculation chains into separate lines and shorten explanations.
If the answer key is too small, increase the answer-key font to at least 8.5 pt and reduce wording elsewhere.
If the answer key is not visually anchored to the bottom, move it down and let the blank space occur between Question 3 and the answer-key rule.
If the page is underfilled, add relevant scenario detail, a useful compact visual, or a meaningful subpart to one question.
If the layout is too crowded, shorten the scenario, condense wording, simplify visuals, or shorten explanations while preserving all non-negotiables.
</revision_rules>
<final_output>
Generate the final worksheet as a polished, black-and-white, one-page PDF file.
</final_output>
